      'Ad revenue is the visible number, but it\'s rarely the whole economic story for a channel run as an actual business. The real cost structure includes production time (or its automation-equivalent cost), API/tooling spend, and the opportunity cost of everything else you didn\'t do with those hours.',
      '## Production cost per video is the number that matters',
      'Whether measured in hours of manual editing or in AI/API cost per rendered video, the relevant unit economic question is: what does one video cost to produce, and does its expected revenue (ad revenue + sponsorships + off-platform monetization) exceed that cost with margin to spare?',
      '## Where automation changes the math',
      'When production cost per video drops from hours of manual work to a few dollars of API cost, the breakeven bar for "is this topic worth making a video about" drops dramatically - topics that wouldn\'t have justified a full manual production cycle become worth testing.',
      '## Diversify revenue, not just content',
      'Ad revenue alone is a volatile, CPM-dependent income stream. Sponsorships, affiliate links, and off-platform products (courses, tools, communities) built on top of an audience are what make a channel resemble an actual business rather than a payout at YouTube\'s mercy.',
      '## The real risk isn\'t the algorithm, it\'s inconsistency',
      'Channels that stop publishing for months lose momentum that\'s expensive to rebuild - the algorithm doesn\'t punish inconsistency directly, but audience habit and session-based discovery both reward channels that keep showing up.',
